{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the garments of the service.</b> I say — according to the plain sense of Scripture — that it is impossible to say it speaks of the priestly garments, since it is written alongside them: ״וְאֶת בִּגְדֵי הַקֹּדֶשׁ לְאַהֲרֹן הַכֹּהֵן וְאֶת בִּגְדֵי בָנָיו לְכַהֵן.״ Rather, these garments of the service are the garments of tekhelet and argaman and tola'at shani mentioned in the passage of the journeys: ״וְנָתְנוּ אֶל בֶּגֶד תְּכֵלֶת וּפָרְשׂוּ עָלָיו בֶּגֶד אַרְגָּמָן וּפָרְשׂוּ עֲלֵיהֶם בֶּגֶד תּוֹלַעַת שָׁנִי״ (Numbers 4). And my words are seen to be correct, for it is written: ״וּמִן הַתְּכֵלֶת וְהָאַרְגָּמָן וְתוֹלַעַת הַשָּׁנִי עָשׂוּ בִּגְדֵי שְׂרָד לְשָׁרֵת בַקֹּדֶשׁ״ (Exodus 39) — and shesh is not mentioned with them; whereas if it spoke of priestly garments, we do not find in any one of them argaman or tola'at shani without shesh." "<b>Garments of the service.</b> Some explain it as a term of labor and service, as its Targum: לְבוּשֵׁי שִׁמּוּשָׁא — but it has no parallel in Scripture. And I say that it is an Aramaic term, like the Targum of קְלָעִים and the Targum of מִכְבָּר, which were woven with a needle made with holes, holes — לצי״דץ בְּלַעַ״ז."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "Rashi derives שרד from the Aramaic root meaning 'to comb/tease,' as in the Targumic renderings of קלעים (curtains) and מכבר (screen), which were made of network — woven with holes; the la'az לצי״דץ appears to be Old French for a needle-worked mesh."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:04.930818630Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}